{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "תרומות", :translit "terumot", :gloss "terumot / heave-offerings"} {:he "כל תרומות הקדשים", :translit "kol terumot ha-kodashim", :gloss "all terumot of sacred things"} {:he "הרי בגלבוע אל טל ואל מטר ושדי תרומות", :translit "harei b'gilboa al tal v'al matar ushedei terumot", :gloss "on the hills of Gilboa, neither dew nor rain, and fields of terumot"} {:he "ואיש תרומות יהרסנה", :translit "v'ish terumot yaharsenah", :gloss "and a man of terumot shall destroy it"} {:he "ושדי תרומות", :translit "ushedei terumot", :gloss "and fields of terumot"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["כל תרומות הקדשים" "ושדי תרומות" "ואיש תרומות יהרסנה"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase הרי בגלבוע אל טל ואל מטר ושדי תרומות appears in II Samuel 1:21, in David's lament over Saul and Jonathan. The phrase איש תרומות יהרסנה appears in Proverbs 11:24 (with yitronot, though the Ba'al HaTurim reads it as terumot). The Sages expounded that a judge who needs nothing from the public resembles a king; this aligns with the verse about Saul, since II Samuel 1:21 mourns the downfall of the king Saul on Gilboa. Conversely, a judge who takes from the public resembles a priest collecting terumot, and such corruption destroys the land.", :sources ["II Samuel 1:21" "Proverbs 11:24"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["כל תרומות הקדשים" "ושדי תרומות" "ואיש תרומות יהרסנה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "כל תרומות הקדשים", :refs ["Numbers 18:19"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ושדי תרומות", :refs ["2 Samuel 1:21"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ואיש תרומות יהרסנה", :refs ["Proverbs 29:4"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:29:56.644342913Z", :model "glm-5.2"}